[
{ch:20,q:"What does the mnemonic OIL RIG stand for?",choices:["Oxidation Is Loss (of electrons); Reduction Is Gain (of electrons)","Oxidation Is Gain (of electrons); Reduction Is Loss (of electrons)","Oxidation Involves Ligands; Reduction Involves Gases","Oxidation Is Light; Reduction Is Gravity"],explain:"OIL RIG: Oxidation Is Loss of electrons, Reduction Is Gain of electrons. Since electrons lost by one species must be gained by another, oxidation and reduction always occur together."},
{ch:20,q:"Why do oxidation and reduction always occur together in a chemical reaction?",choices:["Electrons lost by the species being oxidized must be gained by another species, which is thereby reduced","Oxidation always happens first, and reduction follows afterward in a separate step","Oxygen must always be present for either process to occur","Both processes only occur in aqueous solution"],explain:"Because electrons cannot simply disappear, any electron loss (oxidation) by one species must be matched by an equal electron gain (reduction) by another &ndash; this is why such reactions are called redox reactions."},
{ch:20,q:"For reactions involving no ions at all, such as H<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2HCl(g), how are oxidation and reduction identified?",choices:["By tracking the change in oxidation state of each atom","By tracking which atom loses mass during the reaction","By measuring the pH change of the reaction mixture","Such reactions cannot be classified as redox reactions"],explain:"Since electron transfer cannot be described directly when no ions are formed, oxidation state (the hypothetical ionic charge) is used instead: H's oxidation state increases (oxidized) and Cl's decreases (reduced) in forming HCl."},
{ch:20,q:"In H<sub>2</sub>(g) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &rarr; 2HCl(g), what are the oxidation states of H and Cl in the product HCl?",choices:["H is +1, Cl is &minus;1","H is &minus;1, Cl is +1","H is 0, Cl is 0","H is +1, Cl is +1"],explain:"In the polar covalent product HCl, H is assigned +1 and Cl is assigned &minus;1 (both elements were 0 in H<sub>2</sub> and Cl<sub>2</sub>); since Cl's oxidation state decreased, it is reduced, and since H's increased, it is oxidized."},
{ch:20,q:"What is a disproportionation reaction?",choices:["A reaction in which the oxidation state of the same element both increases and decreases","A reaction in which no element changes oxidation state","A reaction that only occurs between two metals","A reaction in which all reactants are reduced simultaneously"],explain:"A disproportionation reaction is one in which the oxidation state of the same element both increases and decreases, meaning that element is simultaneously oxidized and reduced."},
{ch:20,q:"What is an atom's oxidation state (oxidation number)?",choices:["The charge it would have if the species it belongs to were composed entirely of ions","The number of covalent bonds it forms","Its formal charge in a Lewis structure only","Its position in the periodic table"],explain:"An atom's oxidation state is the charge it would have if the species it belongs to were composed entirely of ions, assigned using a consistent set of rules."},
{ch:20,q:"What oxidation state is assigned to an atom of an uncombined element, such as Fe(s) or O<sub>2</sub>(g)?",choices:["0","+1","&minus;1","It depends on the specific element"],explain:"By convention, an atom of an uncombined (elemental) element is always assigned an oxidation state of 0."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the oxidation state of a monatomic ion, such as Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> or Mg<sup>2+</sup>?",choices:["Equal to its charge","Always 0","Always +1, regardless of the ion's actual charge","Equal to its atomic number"],explain:"The oxidation state of a monatomic ion is simply equal to its charge: &minus;1 for Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>, +2 for Mg<sup>2+</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"In most compounds, what oxidation states are assigned to hydrogen and oxygen?",choices:["Hydrogen is usually +1 and oxygen is usually &minus;2","Hydrogen is usually &minus;1 and oxygen is usually +2","Hydrogen is usually 0 and oxygen is usually &minus;1","Hydrogen is usually +2 and oxygen is usually &minus;1"],explain:"By the standard rules for assigning oxidation states, hydrogen is usually +1 and oxygen is usually &minus;2, and the oxidation states within a species must sum to its overall charge."},
{ch:20,q:"In the complex ion [Cu(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>6</sub>]<sup>2+</sup>, what is the oxidation state of Cu, given NH<sub>3</sub> is a neutral ligand?",choices:["+2","+6","0","+4"],explain:"Since NH<sub>3</sub> is a neutral ligand contributing no charge, the whole complex's +2 charge must equal the metal's oxidation state: Cu = +2."},
{ch:20,q:"In the complex ion [Cr(NH<sub>3</sub>)<sub>4</sub>Cl<sub>2</sub>]<sup>+</sup>, what is the oxidation state of Cr?",choices:["+3","+1","+5","+2"],explain:"With NH<sub>3</sub> neutral and each Cl contributing &minus;1: Cr + (2 &times; &minus;1) = +1, so Cr + (&minus;2) = +1, giving Cr = +3."},
{ch:20,q:"What average oxidation state is calculated for carbon in propane, C<sub>3</sub>H<sub>8</sub>, using H = +1?",choices:["&minus;8/3 (&minus;2&frac23;)","&minus;3","&minus;4","0"],explain:"With H = +1: 8(+1) + 3C = 0, so 3C = &minus;8 and C = &minus;8/3 = &minus;2&frac23;, the average of the two terminal &minus;CH<sub>3</sub> carbons (each &minus;3) and the central &minus;CH<sub>2</sub>&minus; carbon (&minus;2): (2(&minus;3) + (&minus;2))/3 = &minus;8/3."},
{ch:20,q:"How does the Roman numeral in the IUPAC name 'potassium manganate(VII)' (KMnO<sub>4</sub>) relate to oxidation state?",choices:["It indicates the oxidation state of manganese, +7","It indicates the total charge of the compound","It indicates the number of oxygen atoms present","It indicates the oxidation state of potassium"],explain:"Oxidation numbers appear in IUPAC nomenclature as a Roman numeral after the relevant element's name; KMnO<sub>4</sub> is potassium manganate(VII) because Mn has an oxidation state of +7 (K = +1, O = &minus;2 each: +1 + Mn + 4(&minus;2) = 0, so Mn = +7)."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the IUPAC name for Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, in which iron has an oxidation state of +3?",choices:["Iron(III) oxide","Iron(II) oxide","Diiron trioxide","Ferrous oxide(III)"],explain:"Fe<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> is named iron(III) oxide because the oxidation number +3 is shown as a Roman numeral in brackets after the element name."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the IUPAC name for K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>, in which chromium has an oxidation state of +6?",choices:["Potassium dichromate(VI)","Potassium dichromate(VII)","Potassium chromate(III)","Potassium dichromium heptoxide"],explain:"K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub> is potassium dichromate(VI): with K = +1 and O = &minus;2, 2(+1) + 2Cr + 7(&minus;2) = 0 gives 2Cr = 12, so Cr = +6."},
{ch:20,q:"What defines the oxidizing agent in a redox reaction?",choices:["The species that is reduced (it removes electrons from the other reactant)","The species that is oxidized (it supplies electrons to the other reactant)","The species with the largest molar mass","The species present in the greatest concentration"],explain:"The species that is reduced is the oxidizing agent: by gaining electrons itself, it removes electrons from (and so oxidizes) the other reactant."},
{ch:20,q:"What defines the reducing agent in a redox reaction?",choices:["The species that is oxidized (it supplies electrons to the other reactant)","The species that is reduced (it removes electrons from the other reactant)","The species that does not change oxidation state","The species that precipitates out of solution"],explain:"The species that is oxidized is the reducing agent: by losing electrons itself, it supplies electrons to (and so reduces) the other reactant."},
{ch:20,q:"In the reaction of acidified K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>(aq) with FeCl<sub>2</sub>(aq), giving Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) and Cr<sup>3+</sup>(aq), which species is the reducing agent?",choices:["FeCl<sub>2</sub>(aq), since Fe is oxidized from +2 to +3","K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>(aq), since Cr is reduced from +6 to +3","Both FeCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) and K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>(aq) are oxidizing agents","Neither; this is not a redox reaction"],explain:"Fe goes from +2 to +3 (oxidized), so FeCl<sub>2</sub>(aq), the species that is oxidized, is the reducing agent. Cr goes from +6 to +3 (reduced), making K<sub>2</sub>Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub>(aq) the oxidizing agent."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the first step in balancing a redox equation in acidic solution?",choices:["Assign oxidation states to find which element is oxidized and which is reduced","Balance the oxygen atoms using H<sub>2</sub>O","Add H<sup>+</sup> to balance hydrogen atoms","Find the lowest common multiple of the electron counts"],explain:"The first step is to assign oxidation states to identify which element is oxidized and which is reduced, which determines how the two half-equations should be written."},
{ch:20,q:"Once both half-equations are balanced, how are they combined to produce the overall redox equation?",choices:["Find the lowest common multiple of their electron counts, scale each half-equation accordingly, then add and cancel the electrons","Simply add them together without any scaling","Subtract the oxidation half-equation from the reduction half-equation","Multiply the two half-equations together"],explain:"The final step is to find a common multiple of the two electron counts, multiply each half-equation to match it, then add the two half-equations and cancel anything common to both sides, including the electrons."},
{ch:20,q:"In the reaction of Cu(s) with NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) in acidic solution to give NO(g) and Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq), what is the correctly balanced oxidation half-equation?",choices:["Cu(s) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>","Cu(s) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + e<sup>&minus;</sup>","Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Cu(s)","Cu(s) + 2H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g)"],explain:"Copper is oxidized from 0 to +2, losing 2 electrons: Cu(s) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"In the reaction of Cu(s) with NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) in acidic solution to give NO(g), what is the correctly balanced reduction half-equation?",choices:["NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; NO(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 2H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; NO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 3e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; NO(g) + 3O<sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq)","NO(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e<sup>&minus;</sup>"],explain:"N is reduced from +5 to +2, gaining 3 electrons; balancing the 3 O atoms with 2H<sub>2</sub>O and then the 4 H atoms with 4H<sup>+</sup> gives NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; NO(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l)."},
{ch:20,q:"To combine the Cu/NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> half-equations (2e<sup>&minus;</sup> oxidation, 3e<sup>&minus;</sup> reduction) into the overall equation, by what factors must each be multiplied?",choices:["Oxidation by 3, reduction by 2, since the LCM of 2 and 3 is 6","Oxidation by 2, reduction by 3","Oxidation by 6, reduction by 6","Oxidation by 1, reduction by 1"],explain:"The lowest common multiple of 2 and 3 electrons is 6: the oxidation half-equation is multiplied by 3 (giving 6e<sup>&minus;</sup>) and the reduction half-equation is multiplied by 2 (giving 6e<sup>&minus;</sup>), so the electrons cancel exactly when added."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the overall balanced equation for Cu(s) reacting with NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) in acidic solution to give Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) and NO(g)?",choices:["3Cu(s) + 2NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 3Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2NO(g) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","Cu(s) + NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 2H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + NO(g) + H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","3Cu(s) + 2NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; 3Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2NO(g)","2Cu(s) + NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 2Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + NO(g) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l)"],explain:"Multiplying oxidation by 3 and reduction by 2, then adding and cancelling the 6e<sup>&minus;</sup>, gives 3Cu(s) + 2NO<sub>3</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 3Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2NO(g) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)."},
{ch:20,q:"In balancing MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> with Fe<sup>2+</sup> in acidic solution, what change in oxidation state does manganese undergo?",choices:["+7 (in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) to +2 (in Mn<sup>2+</sup>), a gain of 5 electrons","+7 to +4, a gain of 3 electrons","+6 to +2, a gain of 4 electrons","+2 to +7, a loss of 5 electrons"],explain:"Mn goes from +7 in MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> to +2 in Mn<sup>2+</sup>, a gain of 5 electrons, meaning manganese is reduced."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the correctly balanced reduction half-equation for MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) in acidic solution forming Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)?",choices:["MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 5e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 5e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 3e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 5e<sup>&minus;</sup>"],explain:"Balancing the 4 O atoms with 4H<sub>2</sub>O and then the 8 H atoms with 8H<sup>+</sup>, and matching the 5-electron gain, gives MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 5e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)."},
{ch:20,q:"To combine the Fe<sup>2+</sup>/Fe<sup>3+</sup> (1 electron) and MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>/Mn<sup>2+</sup> (5 electron) half-equations, what must be done before adding them?",choices:["The oxidation half-equation must be multiplied by 5, so both involve 5 electrons","The reduction half-equation must be multiplied by 5","Both half-equations must be divided by 5","No scaling is needed; they can be added directly"],explain:"Since oxidation involves 1 electron and reduction involves 5, the lowest common multiple is 5, so the oxidation half-equation (Fe<sup>2+</sup> &rarr; Fe<sup>3+</sup> + e<sup>&minus;</sup>) must be multiplied by 5 to match."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the overall balanced equation for the reaction of MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) with Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) in acidic solution?",choices:["5Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 5Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","5Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; 5Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)","Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 5MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + 5Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)"],explain:"Multiplying the oxidation half-equation by 5 and adding it to the reduction half-equation, cancelling the 5e<sup>&minus;</sup> common to both sides, gives 5Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 5Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l)."},
{ch:20,q:"The equation 5Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + 8H<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 5Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 4H<sub>2</sub>O(l) is the basis for which classic analytical technique?",choices:["A redox titration of acidified KMnO<sub>4</sub>(aq) against Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq)","An acid&ndash;base titration","A gravimetric precipitation analysis","A paper chromatography separation"],explain:"This equation is the basis of a classic redox titration: acidified KMnO<sub>4</sub>(aq) oxidizing Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) to Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq), used to determine the iron content of a sample."},
{ch:20,q:"All the iron in a 2.000 g tablet, dissolved to Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq), required 27.50 cm<sup>3</sup> of 0.100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> KMnO<sub>4</sub>(aq) to reach the endpoint. What amount, in mol, of MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> was used?",choices:["0.00275 mol","0.0275 mol","0.000275 mol","0.0100 mol"],explain:"n(MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) = cV = 0.100 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> &times; (27.50/1000) dm<sup>3</sup> = 0.00275 mol."},
{ch:20,q:"Using n(MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>) = 0.00275 mol and the mole ratio MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> : Fe<sup>2+</sup> = 1 : 5, what amount, in mol, of Fe<sup>2+</sup> was present?",choices:["0.01375 mol","0.00275 mol","0.000550 mol","0.0550 mol"],explain:"Since the mole ratio is 1 : 5, n(Fe<sup>2+</sup>) = 5 &times; 0.00275 = 0.01375 mol."},
{ch:20,q:"Given n(Fe) = 0.01375 mol and M(Fe) = 55.85 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, what mass of iron was in the 2.000 g tablet, and what is the percentage by mass of iron?",choices:["0.768 g, which is 38.4% of the tablet's mass","0.768 g, which is 76.8% of the tablet's mass","1.375 g, which is 68.8% of the tablet's mass","0.384 g, which is 19.2% of the tablet's mass"],explain:"m(Fe) = n &times; M = 0.01375 &times; 55.85 = 0.768 g. %Fe = (0.768/2.000) &times; 100 = 38.4%."},
{ch:20,q:"In the iodine&ndash;thiosulfate method, excess iodide is first converted to iodine by an oxidizing agent, then the liberated iodine is titrated against which standard solution?",choices:["Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(aq)","NaOH(aq)","HCl(aq)","KMnO<sub>4</sub>(aq)"],explain:"The liberated iodine is titrated against standard sodium thiosulfate solution: 2S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) + I<sub>2</sub>(aq) &rarr; 2I<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + S<sub>4</sub>O<sub>6</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq)."},
{ch:20,q:"In a back-titration, a 10.00 cm<sup>3</sup> sample of bleach (OCl<sup>&minus;</sup>) reacted with excess iodide, and the liberated I<sub>2</sub> required 38.65 cm<sup>3</sup> of 0.0200 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> Na<sub>2</sub>S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>(aq). What amount, in mol, of S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> was used?",choices:["7.73 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol","3.865 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol","1.93 &times; 10<sup>&minus;3</sup> mol","2.00 &times; 10<sup>&minus;2</sup> mol"],explain:"n(S<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>) = cV = 0.0200 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> &times; (38.65/1000) dm<sup>3</sup> = 7.73 &times; 10<sup>&minus;4</sup> mol."},
{ch:20,q:"How does oxidizing strength vary down group 17 (the halogens)?",choices:["It decreases down the group: F<sub>2</sub> &gt; Cl<sub>2</sub> &gt; Br<sub>2</sub> &gt; I<sub>2</sub>","It increases down the group: I<sub>2</sub> &gt; Br<sub>2</sub> &gt; Cl<sub>2</sub> &gt; F<sub>2</sub>","It remains constant down the group","It alternates between increasing and decreasing"],explain:"The halogens' ability to act as oxidizing agents (gaining electrons to form halide ions) decreases down group 17: F<sub>2</sub> &gt; Cl<sub>2</sub> &gt; Br<sub>2</sub> &gt; I<sub>2</sub> in oxidizing strength."},
{ch:20,q:"What is observed when zinc metal is added to CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq)?",choices:["Zinc displaces copper: Zn(s) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu(s)","No reaction occurs, since zinc is a weaker reducing agent than copper","Copper displaces zinc: Cu(s) + Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Zn(s)","Both metals dissolve without forming any solid product"],explain:"Zinc, a more reactive metal and stronger reducing agent than copper, displaces copper from solution: Zn(s) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu(s)."},
{ch:20,q:"Why does no reaction occur when copper metal is added to Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)?",choices:["Copper is too weak a reducing agent to force electrons onto Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)","Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is not soluble in water","Copper metal does not conduct electricity","Zinc ions cannot be reduced under any circumstances"],explain:"Copper is a weaker reducing agent than zinc, so it cannot force electrons onto Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) to reduce it; the reverse direction (Zn reducing Cu<sup>2+</sup>) is the one that actually proceeds."},
{ch:20,q:"Why do reactive metals such as zinc release H<sub>2</sub>(g) when added to dilute acid?",choices:["The metal reduces H<sup>+</sup>(aq) to H<sub>2</sub>(g) while itself being oxidized","The metal oxidizes H<sup>+</sup>(aq), releasing O<sub>2</sub>(g)","H<sub>2</sub>(g) is released by simple physical dissolution, with no electron transfer","The acid is reduced to form a metal oxide"],explain:"Reactive metals act as reducing agents, donating electrons to reduce H<sup>+</sup>(aq) to H<sub>2</sub>(g): 2H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Zn(s) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + H<sub>2</sub>(g), while the metal itself is oxidized."},
{ch:20,q:"Using the reactivity series Mg &gt; Al &gt; Zn &gt; Fe &gt; Pb &gt; Cu &gt; Ag (strongest to weakest reducing agent), will 2FeCl<sub>3</sub>(aq) + 3Mg(s) &rarr; 3MgCl<sub>2</sub>(aq) + 2Fe(s) occur?",choices:["Yes, since Mg is a stronger reducing agent than Fe","No, since Mg is a weaker reducing agent than Fe","Yes, but only in the solid state","No, since this reaction requires a catalyst"],explain:"Since Mg is a stronger reducing agent than Fe in the given series, Mg(s) can reduce Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq) to Fe(s), so this reaction will occur spontaneously."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the function of a voltaic (galvanic) cell?",choices:["It physically separates the oxidation and reduction half-reactions of a spontaneous redox reaction, converting chemical energy into electrical energy","It uses an external power source to drive a non-spontaneous reaction","It converts electrical energy directly into heat with no chemical change","It combines oxidation and reduction into a single half-cell"],explain:"A voltaic cell physically separates the oxidation and reduction half-reactions of a spontaneous redox reaction, forcing electrons through an external circuit and converting chemical energy into electrical energy."},
{ch:20,q:"In a simple zinc half-cell, Zn(s) &#8652; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>, what is true of a more reactive metal's electrode compared to a less reactive one?",choices:["This equilibrium favours ion formation more strongly, building up a more negative charge on the electrode","This equilibrium favours ion formation less strongly, building up a more positive charge on the electrode","The equilibrium position is identical for all metals","No equilibrium is established for reactive metals"],explain:"The more reactive the metal, the further the equilibrium Zn(s) &#8652; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> favours ion formation, and the more negative the charge built up on that electrode relative to a less reactive metal."},
{ch:20,q:"In a zinc&ndash;copper voltaic cell, what is the role of the salt bridge?",choices:["It completes the circuit by allowing ion migration, neutralizing charge build-up in each half-cell, without allowing the two solutions to mix freely","It allows electrons to flow directly between the two beakers","It provides the electrical energy that drives the reaction","It acts as a catalyst to speed up the overall reaction"],explain:"The salt bridge completes the circuit: anions drift towards the zinc half-cell and cations towards the copper half-cell, neutralizing the charge build-up in each beaker, without the two solutions mixing freely."},
{ch:20,q:"In a zinc&ndash;copper voltaic cell, what happens at the zinc electrode (the anode)?",choices:["Zn(s) is oxidized to Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq), releasing electrons into the external circuit","Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is reduced to Zn(s), accepting electrons from the circuit","Zn(s) reacts directly with Cu(s) at the electrode surface","No chemical change occurs at the zinc electrode"],explain:"At the zinc electrode (anode, negative), Zn(s) is oxidized to Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq), releasing electrons into the wire, which flow to the copper electrode."},
{ch:20,q:"In a zinc&ndash;copper voltaic cell, what happens at the copper electrode (the cathode)?",choices:["Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is reduced and deposits as Cu(s)","Cu(s) is oxidized to Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq)","No reaction occurs at the copper electrode","H<sub>2</sub>(g) is produced at the copper electrode"],explain:"At the copper electrode (cathode, positive), electrons arriving through the wire reduce Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq), which deposits as solid Cu(s)."},
{ch:20,q:"In which direction do electrons flow through the external circuit of a voltaic cell?",choices:["From anode to cathode","From cathode to anode","Electrons do not flow through the external circuit; only ions move","Electrons oscillate back and forth between electrodes"],explain:"Electrons flow from the anode (where oxidation releases them) to the cathode (where reduction consumes them) through the external circuit."},
{ch:20,q:"What happens to a voltaic cell's current and voltage if the salt bridge is removed?",choices:["The circuit is incomplete, and no current or voltage is generated","The voltage increases significantly","The current flow reverses direction","Nothing changes; the salt bridge is optional"],explain:"Without a salt bridge, the circuit is incomplete, since there is no pathway for ions to balance the charge building up in each half-cell, so no current or voltage is generated."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why can a voltmeter not measure the tendency of a single half-cell to be reduced in isolation?",choices:["A voltmeter can only measure the potential difference generated once a half-cell is connected to another half-cell","Voltmeters are only accurate for solid-state electrodes, not solutions","Half-cells never generate any measurable potential alone","A voltmeter can only be used with molten electrolytes"],explain:"A voltmeter measures only a potential difference, so an isolated half-cell's tendency to be reduced cannot be measured directly; all half-cells must be compared against a common, universally agreed reference."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the universally agreed reference used to measure standard electrode potentials?",choices:["The standard hydrogen electrode (SHE)","The standard copper electrode","The standard calomel electrode","The standard oxygen electrode"],explain:"To compare half-cells on a common scale, all are measured against the standard hydrogen electrode (SHE), a single, universally agreed reference."},
{ch:20,q:"What are the standard conditions at the standard hydrogen electrode?",choices:["1.0 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> H<sup>+</sup>(aq), H<sub>2</sub>(g) at 100 kPa, platinized platinum electrode, 298 K","0.1 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> H<sup>+</sup>(aq), H<sub>2</sub>(g) at 1 kPa, copper electrode, 273 K","1.0 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> H<sup>+</sup>(aq), O<sub>2</sub>(g) at 100 kPa, platinum electrode, 298 K","10.0 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> H<sup>+</sup>(aq), H<sub>2</sub>(g) at 200 kPa, platinum electrode, 373 K"],explain:"The SHE consists of a platinized platinum electrode immersed in 1.0 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> H<sup>+</sup>(aq) with H<sub>2</sub>(g) bubbled over it at 100 kPa and 298 K, establishing the equilibrium 2H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &#8652; H<sub>2</sub>(g)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What numerical electrode potential is assigned, by convention, to the standard hydrogen electrode?",choices:["Exactly 0.00 V","Exactly 1.00 V","Exactly &minus;1.00 V","It varies with temperature"],explain:"By convention, the standard hydrogen electrode is assigned an electrode potential of exactly 0.00 V, serving as the zero point of the electrochemical series."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the standard electrode potential, E&deg;, of a half-cell?",choices:["The emf generated when that half-cell is connected to the SHE by an external circuit and salt bridge, under standard conditions","The voltage measured when the half-cell is connected to any arbitrary other half-cell","The current flowing through the half-cell at 298 K","The concentration of ions present in the half-cell at equilibrium"],explain:"The standard electrode potential, E&deg;, of a half-cell is the emf generated when that half-cell is connected to the standard hydrogen electrode under standard conditions: 1.0 mol dm<sup>&minus;3</sup> solutions, 100 kPa gases, pure substances, 298 K."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: When the SHE is connected to a Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Cu(s) half-cell, the voltmeter reads +0.34 V. What does this positive reading indicate?",choices:["Electrons flow from the hydrogen half-cell to the copper half-cell, so H<sub>2</sub>(g) is oxidized and Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is reduced","Electrons flow from the copper half-cell to the hydrogen half-cell","No electron flow occurs between the two half-cells","Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) has a lower tendency to be reduced than H<sup>+</sup>(aq)"],explain:"The positive reading shows electrons flow from the hydrogen half-cell to the copper half-cell: H<sub>2</sub>(g) is oxidized (anode) and Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is reduced (cathode), since Cu<sup>2+</sup> has a greater tendency to be reduced than H<sup>+</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: When the SHE is connected to a Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Zn(s) half-cell, the reading is &minus;0.76 V. What does this indicate?",choices:["Zn(s) is oxidized (anode) and H<sup>+</sup>(aq) is reduced (cathode)","Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is reduced (cathode) and H<sub>2</sub>(g) is oxidized (anode)","No reaction occurs between zinc and the SHE","Zinc has a lower reactivity than hydrogen"],explain:"The &minus;0.76 V reading shows electrons flow from the zinc half-cell to the hydrogen half-cell, so Zn(s) is oxidized (anode) and H<sup>+</sup>(aq) is reduced (cathode), consistent with zinc's higher reactivity."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why are all tabulated E&deg; values written specifically for the reduction direction, even though a species may actually be oxidized in a particular cell?",choices:["Because the SHE is always connected to the negative terminal of the voltmeter by convention","Because oxidation potentials cannot be measured experimentally","Because all species are always reduced in practice, never oxidized","Because reduction always releases more energy than oxidation"],explain:"Because the SHE is always connected to the negative terminal of the voltmeter by convention, every tabulated E&deg; value refers specifically to the reduction half-reaction, hence the alternative name 'standard reduction potential'."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Are E&deg; values scaled up or down to match the stoichiometric coefficients used in a particular overall reaction?",choices:["No, E&deg; values are independent of the stoichiometric coefficients used to balance a half-equation","Yes, E&deg; values must always be multiplied by the same factor as the half-equation","Yes, but only if the coefficient is greater than 2","E&deg; values are always divided by the number of electrons transferred"],explain:"E&deg; values are independent of the stoichiometric coefficients used to balance a half-equation; they are never scaled up or down to match a particular reaction, unlike &Delta;G&deg; (which does depend on n)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What does a more positive E&deg; value indicate about a half-cell?",choices:["That half-cell is more readily reduced","That half-cell is more readily oxidized","That half-cell has a higher resistance","That half-cell contains a stronger acid"],explain:"The more positive the E&deg; value, the more readily that half-cell is reduced (i.e. the stronger the oxidizing agent the species on the left of the half-equation is)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In a voltaic cell, electrons in the external circuit always flow from the half-cell with which E&deg; value towards the half-cell with which E&deg; value?",choices:["From the more negative (lower) E&deg; towards the more positive (higher) E&deg;","From the more positive (higher) E&deg; towards the more negative (lower) E&deg;","Direction depends only on the concentrations used, never on E&deg;","Electrons flow equally in both directions at equilibrium"],explain:"Electrons always flow, through the external circuit of a voltaic cell, from the half-cell with the more negative (lower) E&deg; towards the half-cell with the more positive (higher) E&deg;."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the electrochemical series?",choices:["A ranking of half-cells by their E&deg; values","A ranking of elements by their atomic radius","A list of elements ordered by electronegativity only","A table of boiling points for common electrolytes"],explain:"Ranking half-cells by their standard electrode potentials produces the electrochemical series, extending the simple metal activity series to any species that can be oxidized or reduced."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What advantage does the electrochemical series have over the simple metal activity series?",choices:["It extends to any species that can be oxidized or reduced, including non-metals and polyatomic ions such as MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> and Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>","It only applies to Group 1 metals","It cannot be used to predict spontaneity of any reaction","It applies exclusively to halogens"],explain:"The electrochemical series extends the simple metal activity series to any species that can be oxidized or reduced, including non-metals, their ions, and polyatomic ions such as MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> and Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Using the electrochemical series (Li<sup>+</sup>/Li = &minus;3.04 V, F<sub>2</sub>/F<sup>&minus;</sup> = +2.87 V), which represents the strongest reducing agent and which the strongest oxidizing agent?",choices:["Li(s) is the strongest reducing agent; F<sub>2</sub>(g) is the strongest oxidizing agent","F<sub>2</sub>(g) is the strongest reducing agent; Li(s) is the strongest oxidizing agent","Li<sup>+</sup>(aq) is the strongest reducing agent; F<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is the strongest oxidizing agent","Both are equally strong oxidizing agents"],explain:"The most negative E&deg; values belong to the strongest reducing agents (most easily oxidized, like Li(s)); the most positive E&deg; values belong to the strongest oxidizing agents (most easily reduced, like F<sub>2</sub>(g))."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the correct equation for calculating standard cell potential, E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>?",choices:["E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub>","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub>","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> + E&deg;<sub>anode</sub>","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> &times; E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub>"],explain:"E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>(reduction half-cell, cathode)</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>(reduction half-cell, anode)</sub>, using both tabulated reduction potentials exactly as given, including sign."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: When calculating E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub>, should either E&deg; value ever be reversed in sign before subtracting?",choices:["No, both E&deg; values used are the tabulated reduction potentials, taken exactly as given, including sign, never reversed before subtracting","Yes, the anode's E&deg; must always be reversed in sign first","Yes, the cathode's E&deg; must always be reversed in sign first","Both values must be reversed in sign before subtracting"],explain:"Both E&deg; values used in the E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> equation are the tabulated reduction potentials, taken exactly as given, including sign, and are never reversed before subtracting, even though the anode is actually being oxidized."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In the E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> calculation, which half-cell is identified as the cathode?",choices:["The half-cell with the higher (more positive) E&deg; value","The half-cell with the lower (more negative) E&deg; value","The half-cell with the larger electrode surface area","The half-cell containing the metal with lower density"],explain:"The half-cell with the higher (more positive) E&deg; is reduced and is the cathode; the half-cell with the lower (more negative) E&deg; is forced into oxidation and is the anode."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For the spontaneous direction of any proposed redox reaction, what sign will the correctly calculated E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> always have?",choices:["Positive","Negative","Zero","It depends on temperature, not on which reaction is spontaneous"],explain:"Subtracting the lower E&deg; (anode) from the higher E&deg; (cathode) always gives a positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for the reaction that actually happens spontaneously."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: If a calculated E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for a proposed reaction comes out negative, what does this indicate?",choices:["The reaction as written is non-spontaneous; the reverse reaction is spontaneous instead, with the same magnitude but positive sign","The reaction as written proceeds extremely fast","The reaction cannot be analysed using E&deg; values at all","The half-cells involved must both have negative E&deg; values individually"],explain:"A negative E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> means the reaction as written is non-spontaneous; the reverse reaction is spontaneous instead, with a cell potential of the same magnitude but positive sign."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For a voltaic cell built from Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Zn(s) (E&deg; = &minus;0.76 V) and Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Cu(s) (E&deg; = +0.34 V), which half-cell is the cathode?",choices:["Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Cu(s), since copper has the higher (more positive) E&deg;","Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Zn(s), since zinc has the lower E&deg;","Neither; both act as anodes simultaneously","It cannot be determined without the actual concentrations used"],explain:"Copper has the higher (more positive) E&deg; (+0.34 V vs &minus;0.76 V), so Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is reduced at the cathode; zinc, with the lower E&deg;, is oxidized at the anode."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Calculate E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for the zinc&ndash;copper voltaic cell: Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Zn(s), E&deg; = &minus;0.76 V (anode); Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Cu(s), E&deg; = +0.34 V (cathode).",choices:["+1.10 V","&minus;1.10 V","+0.42 V","&minus;0.42 V"],explain:"E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> = (+0.34) &minus; (&minus;0.76) = +0.34 + 0.76 = +1.10 V."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For the zinc&ndash;copper voltaic cell with E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +1.10 V, what is the overall spontaneous reaction?",choices:["Zn(s) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu(s)","Cu(s) + Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Zn(s)","Zn(s) + Cu(s) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq)","2Zn(s) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; 2Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu(s)"],explain:"With Zn(s) oxidized at the anode and Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) reduced at the cathode, the overall spontaneous reaction is Zn(s) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu(s), consistent with the positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Acidified potassium manganate(VII) is mixed with concentrated HCl, producing Cl<sub>2</sub>(g). Given MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>/Mn<sup>2+</sup>, E&deg; = +1.51 V, and Cl<sub>2</sub>/Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>, E&deg; = +1.36 V, which is the stronger oxidizing agent?",choices:["MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), since it has the higher E&deg;","Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), since it has the higher E&deg;","Both are equally strong oxidizing agents","Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), since it has the lower E&deg;"],explain:"MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>/Mn<sup>2+</sup> has the higher E&deg; (+1.51 V vs +1.36 V), so MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is the stronger oxidizing agent; it is reduced (cathode), while Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized (anode)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Calculate E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) oxidizing Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) to Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), given MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>/Mn<sup>2+</sup>, E&deg; = +1.51 V (cathode), and Cl<sub>2</sub>/Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>, E&deg; = +1.36 V (anode).",choices:["+0.15 V","&minus;0.15 V","+2.87 V","+0.08 V"],explain:"E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> = (+1.51) &minus; (+1.36) = +0.15 V, confirming MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) can spontaneously oxidize Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) to Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) under standard conditions."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Use E&deg; values to decide whether Ni(s) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Ni<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Mn(s) occurs spontaneously, given Ni<sup>2+</sup>/Ni, E&deg; = &minus;0.26 V, and Mn<sup>2+</sup>/Mn, E&deg; = &minus;1.18 V.",choices:["It does not occur spontaneously; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for this proposed direction is &minus;0.92 V","It occurs spontaneously; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for this proposed direction is +0.92 V","It occurs spontaneously; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for this proposed direction is +1.44 V","It does not occur; this pair of half-cells cannot be combined"],explain:"In the proposed direction, Ni(s) is 'oxidized' (treated as anode) and Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is 'reduced' (treated as cathode): E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>(Mn<sup>2+</sup>/Mn)</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>(Ni<sup>2+</sup>/Ni)</sub> = (&minus;1.18) &minus; (&minus;0.26) = &minus;0.92 V, confirming this proposed reaction is not spontaneous."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Given Ni<sup>2+</sup>/Ni, E&deg; = &minus;0.26 V, and Mn<sup>2+</sup>/Mn, E&deg; = &minus;1.18 V, what is the spontaneous direction and its E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>?",choices:["Ni<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Mn(s) &rarr; Ni(s) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq), E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.92 V","Ni(s) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Ni<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Mn(s), E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.92 V","Ni<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Mn(s) &rarr; Ni(s) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq), E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;0.92 V","Neither direction is spontaneous"],explain:"Since Ni<sup>2+</sup>/Ni has the higher (less negative) E&deg;, Ni<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is reduced (cathode) and Mn(s) is oxidized (anode) in the spontaneous direction: E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = (&minus;0.26) &minus; (&minus;1.18) = +0.92 V, for Ni<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Mn(s) &rarr; Ni(s) + Mn<sup>2+</sup>(aq)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why do the two directions of the Ni/Mn reaction give E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> values of exactly the same magnitude but opposite sign (&minus;0.92 V and +0.92 V)?",choices:["Reversing which half-cell is treated as cathode and which as anode simply swaps which term is subtracted from which, negating the result","Because E&deg; values for Ni and Mn are coincidentally equal in magnitude","Because the Faraday constant changes sign when the reaction is reversed","This is a calculation error; both directions should give the same sign"],explain:"Swapping which half-cell is cathode and which is anode swaps the two terms in E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub>, which simply negates the result: (&minus;1.18) &minus; (&minus;0.26) = &minus;0.92, while (&minus;0.26) &minus; (&minus;1.18) = +0.92."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Will acidified potassium dichromate(VI) oxidize Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) to Fe<sup>3+</sup>(aq)? Given Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>/Cr<sup>3+</sup>, E&deg; = +1.33 V, and Fe<sup>3+</sup>/Fe<sup>2+</sup>, E&deg; = +0.77 V.",choices:["Yes, E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.56 V, so the reaction is spontaneous","No, E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;0.56 V, so the reaction is non-spontaneous","Yes, E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +2.10 V","No, dichromate cannot oxidize iron(II) under any conditions"],explain:"Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>/Cr<sup>3+</sup> has the higher E&deg;, so it is reduced (cathode); Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq) is oxidized (anode). E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = (+1.33) &minus; (+0.77) = +0.56 V, confirming the reaction is spontaneous, consistent with dichromate's use as an oxidizing titrant for iron(II)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why is it unnecessary to memorize a simple metal activity series when E&deg; data is available?",choices:["Because electrons always tend to flow towards the half-cell with the higher E&deg; value, so E&deg; data can predict spontaneity for any proposed reaction, including between non-metal or polyatomic-ion systems","Because E&deg; data only applies to Group 1 and Group 2 metals","Because the activity series and E&deg; data always disagree with each other","Because spontaneity cannot be predicted from E&deg; values alone"],explain:"Because electrons always tend to flow towards the half-cell with the higher E&deg; value, E&deg; data can predict whether any proposed redox reaction &ndash; between metals, non-metals, or polyatomic ions &ndash; will occur spontaneously, without needing to memorize an activity series."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Does a positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> give any information about how fast a reaction will proceed?",choices:["No, E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> answers only 'how far?', not 'how fast?'; a spontaneous reaction with high activation energy may proceed unobservably slowly","Yes, a larger positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> always means a faster reaction rate","Yes, but only for reactions involving metals, not non-metals","No, but a negative E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> always means a fast reverse reaction"],explain:"E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> answers only 'how far?' (whether a reaction is energetically favourable), giving no information on 'how fast?'; a reaction predicted to be spontaneous may proceed at an unobservably slow rate if its activation energy is high."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Do E&deg; values strictly apply under any conditions of temperature, concentration, and pressure?",choices:["No, E&deg; values strictly apply only under standard conditions; changing these can alter them, occasionally reversing which direction is spontaneous","Yes, E&deg; values are universal constants unaffected by any conditions","E&deg; values only change with temperature, never with concentration or pressure","E&deg; values only apply to gaseous half-cells"],explain:"E&deg; values strictly apply only under standard conditions; changing temperature, concentration, or pressure can alter them, occasionally even reversing which direction is spontaneous."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the relationship between a positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and the standard Gibbs energy change, &Delta;G&deg;?",choices:["A positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> corresponds to a negative &Delta;G&deg;, both indicating a spontaneous reaction","A positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> corresponds to a positive &Delta;G&deg;","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and &Delta;G&deg; are unrelated quantities","A positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> always corresponds to &Delta;G&deg; = 0"],explain:"A positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> corresponds to a spontaneous, energetically favourable reaction &ndash; exactly the condition for a negative standard Gibbs energy change, &Delta;G&deg;."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the correct equation linking &Delta;G&deg; and E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>?",choices:["&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>","&Delta;G&deg; = nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>","&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>/F","&Delta;G&deg; = F/(nE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>)"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, where n is the number of moles of electrons transferred in the balanced equation and F is the Faraday constant, directly linking cell potential to Gibbs energy."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, what does the symbol n represent?",choices:["The number of moles of electrons transferred in the balanced overall equation","The number of half-cells used in the reaction","The total number of atoms in the balanced equation","The number of moles of the limiting reactant"],explain:"In &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, n is the number of moles of electrons transferred in the balanced overall equation, determined from the half-equations."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the value of the Faraday constant, F?",choices:["96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","6.022 &times; 10<sup>23</sup> C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","1.602 &times; 10<sup>&minus;19</sup> C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","8.31 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"The Faraday constant, the charge on one mole of electrons, is F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> (often rounded to 96 500 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For the zinc&ndash;copper cell, Zn(s) + Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + Cu(s), how many moles of electrons, n, are transferred in the balanced equation?",choices:["2","1","3","4"],explain:"From the half-equations Zn(s) &rarr; Zn<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> and Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Cu(s), n = 2 mol of electrons are transferred."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Calculate &Delta;G&deg; at 298 K for the zinc&ndash;copper cell, where n = 2 mol, F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, and E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +1.10 V.",choices:["&minus;212 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+212 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;106 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;193 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;(2)(96 485)(1.10) = &minus;212 267 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; &minus;212 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What does the large negative &Delta;G&deg; (&minus;212 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) calculated for the zinc&ndash;copper cell confirm?",choices:["A strongly spontaneous, energetically favourable reaction, consistent with the large positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>","A reaction that is only slightly spontaneous","A non-spontaneous reaction requiring external energy input","A reaction at equilibrium, since &Delta;G&deg; is non-zero"],explain:"The large negative &Delta;G&deg; confirms a strongly spontaneous, energetically favourable reaction, consistent with the large positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> of +1.10 V for this cell."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: A reaction has n = 4 mol of electrons transferred and E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.50 V. Using F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, what is &Delta;G&deg;, to 3 significant figures?",choices:["&minus;193 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+193 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;48.2 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;386 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;(4)(96 485)(0.50) = &minus;192 970 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; &minus;193 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: A reaction has n = 1 mol of electrons transferred and E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;0.30 V. Using F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, what is &Delta;G&deg;, to 3 significant figures, and what does its sign indicate?",choices:["+29.0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, indicating the reaction as written is non-spontaneous","&minus;29.0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, indicating the reaction as written is spontaneous","+28 965 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, indicating a highly spontaneous reaction","0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, indicating the reaction is at equilibrium"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;(1)(96 485)(&minus;0.30) = +28 946 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; +29.0 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>; the positive value confirms the reaction as written is non-spontaneous, consistent with its negative E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the correct sign relationship linking E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, &Delta;G&deg;, and spontaneity?",choices:["E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> positive &harr; &Delta;G&deg; negative &harr; spontaneous as written","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> positive &harr; &Delta;G&deg; positive &harr; spontaneous as written","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> negative &harr; &Delta;G&deg; negative &harr; spontaneous as written","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and &Delta;G&deg; always have the same sign"],explain:"E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> positive corresponds to &Delta;G&deg; negative, both indicating the reaction is spontaneous as written; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> negative corresponds to &Delta;G&deg; positive, indicating a non-spontaneous reaction (the reverse is spontaneous instead)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What happens to &Delta;G&deg; as E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> becomes more positive?",choices:["&Delta;G&deg; becomes more negative, and the reaction becomes more energetically favourable","&Delta;G&deg; becomes more positive","&Delta;G&deg; remains unchanged","&Delta;G&deg; approaches zero"],explain:"The more positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, the more negative &Delta;G&deg;, and the more energetically favourable the reaction; a voltmeter can be thought of as an indirect measure of Gibbs energy change."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Besides &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, what other two equations relate &Delta;G&deg; to spontaneity or equilibrium, giving three mathematically consistent routes to the same energetics?",choices:["&Delta;G&deg; related directly to spontaneity (Reactivity 1.4), and &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K (Reactivity 3.1)","&Delta;G&deg; = mc&Delta;T and &Delta;G&deg; = PV","&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub> is the only equation for &Delta;G&deg;; no other routes exist","&Delta;G&deg; = &Delta;H + T&Delta;S only, with no link to K or E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>"],explain:"Reactivity 1.4 related &Delta;G&deg; to spontaneity directly, and Reactivity 3.1 related it to the equilibrium constant via &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;RT ln K; this chapter adds a third, equivalent route specific to redox reactions, &Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub>."},
{ch:20,q:"What is an electrolytic cell?",choices:["A cell that uses an external electrical supply to drive non-spontaneous oxidation and reduction, converting electrical energy to chemical energy","A cell that generates electrical energy spontaneously from a chemical reaction","A cell that only operates with molten electrolytes, never aqueous solutions","A cell identical to a voltaic cell in every respect"],explain:"An electrolytic cell reverses a voltaic cell: an external electrical supply drives non-spontaneous oxidation and reduction, converting electrical energy to chemical energy &ndash; this process is called electrolysis."},
{ch:20,q:"In the electrolysis of a molten salt such as NaCl(l), why does exactly one ion type migrate to, and react at, each electrode?",choices:["Because only the compound's own ions are present in a molten salt","Because water is always present to compete for discharge","Because molten salts contain no mobile ions at all","Because electrode material always determines the product, not the ions present"],explain:"In a molten salt, only the compound's own ions are present (no water to compete), so exactly one ion type migrates to, and reacts at, each electrode: cations reduced at the cathode, anions oxidized at the anode."},
{ch:20,q:"In the electrolysis of molten NaCl, what occurs at the cathode?",choices:["Na<sup>+</sup>(l) + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Na(l)","2Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(l) &rarr; Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>","2H<sup>+</sup>(l) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>(g)","Na(l) &rarr; Na<sup>+</sup>(l) + e<sup>&minus;</sup>"],explain:"At the cathode (negative electrode), Na<sup>+</sup>(l) is reduced: Na<sup>+</sup>(l) + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Na(l), and molten sodium collects."},
{ch:20,q:"In the electrolysis of molten NaCl, what occurs at the anode, and what is the overall equation?",choices:["2Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(l) &rarr; Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>; overall 2NaCl(l) &rarr; 2Na(l) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g)","Na<sup>+</sup>(l) + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Na(l); overall 2NaCl(l) &rarr; 2Na(l) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g)","2Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(l) &rarr; Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>; overall NaCl(l) &rarr; Na(l) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g)","O<sup>2&minus;</sup> is oxidized at the anode instead of Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>"],explain:"At the anode (positive electrode), Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(l) is oxidized: 2Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(l) &rarr; Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>; overall, 2NaCl(l) &rarr; 2Na(l) + Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), splitting a stable compound into its reactive elements."},
{ch:20,q:"In the Hall&ndash;H&eacute;roult process, why is Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> dissolved in molten cryolite (Na<sub>3</sub>AlF<sub>6</sub>) rather than electrolyzed on its own?",choices:["Cryolite lowers the mixture's melting point and raises its conductivity, substantially cutting energy costs","Cryolite reacts with Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> to directly form aluminium metal","Pure Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> cannot conduct electricity even when molten","Cryolite prevents any oxygen gas from being formed at the anode"],explain:"Dissolving Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub> in molten cryolite lowers the mixture's melting point and raises its conductivity compared with pure molten Al<sub>2</sub>O<sub>3</sub>, substantially cutting the energy costs of the process."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why is predicting electrolysis products harder for an aqueous solution than for a molten salt?",choices:["Water itself can be oxidized or reduced at either electrode, competing with the dissolved ions","Aqueous solutions never conduct electricity","Aqueous solutions always produce only hydrogen and oxygen gas","Molten salts always contain more ion types than aqueous solutions"],explain:"Water itself can be oxidized or reduced at either electrode, competing with the dissolved ions; this competition, called selective discharge, is absent in a molten salt where only the compound's own ions are present."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is 'selective discharge' in the context of aqueous electrolysis?",choices:["The phenomenon where either the dissolved ion or water itself is preferentially oxidized or reduced at an electrode","The process of removing ions from solution before electrolysis begins","The selective dissolution of the electrode material into solution","A technique for selecting which electrode acts as cathode"],explain:"Selective discharge refers to the competition at each electrode between the dissolved ion (M<sup>+</sup> or A<sup>&minus;</sup>) and water itself for oxidation or reduction, with the actual product depending on which is discharged preferentially."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: At the cathode of an aqueous electrolysis, which half-reaction actually occurs, according to E&deg; data?",choices:["The half-reaction with the higher E&deg; (it is reduced preferentially)","The half-reaction with the lower E&deg; (it is reduced preferentially)","Both half-reactions occur simultaneously in equal amounts","The half-reaction requiring less current"],explain:"At the cathode, the half-reaction with the higher E&deg; is the one that actually occurs, since it is reduced preferentially over a half-reaction with a lower (less favourable) E&deg;."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: At the anode of an aqueous electrolysis, which half-reaction actually occurs, according to E&deg; data?",choices:["The half-reaction with the lower E&deg; (it is oxidized preferentially)","The half-reaction with the higher E&deg; (it is oxidized preferentially)","Both half-reactions occur simultaneously in equal amounts","The half-reaction requiring the most voltage"],explain:"At the anode, the half-reaction with the lower E&deg; is the one that actually occurs, since it is oxidized preferentially (a lower E&deg; reduction potential corresponds to a greater tendency toward oxidation)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Besides E&deg;, what two other factors can affect which product forms during aqueous electrolysis?",choices:["The relative concentration of the ions, and the nature of the electrode (inert vs reactive)","The colour of the solution, and the size of the container","The atomic radius of the ions, and the ambient air pressure","The pH of pure water only, and the electrode's melting point"],explain:"Besides E&deg;, the relative concentration of the ions present and the nature of the electrode (inert vs reactive) can also affect which product actually forms during electrolysis."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In the electrolysis of dilute NaOH(aq), comparing Na<sup>+</sup>/Na (E&deg; = &minus;2.71 V) with H<sub>2</sub>O/H<sub>2</sub> (E&deg; = &minus;0.83 V) at the cathode, which is reduced preferentially?",choices:["H<sub>2</sub>O(l), since it has the higher E&deg;, producing H<sub>2</sub>(g)","Na<sup>+</sup>(aq), since it has the higher E&deg;, producing Na(s)","Both are reduced in exactly equal amounts","Neither; no reduction occurs at the cathode"],explain:"Water has the higher E&deg; (&minus;0.83 V vs &minus;2.71 V for Na<sup>+</sup>), so it is reduced preferentially: 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), giving H<sub>2</sub>(g) at the cathode."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In the electrolysis of dilute NaOH(aq), comparing the OH<sup>&minus;</sup>/H<sub>2</sub>O/O<sub>2</sub> couple (E&deg; = +0.40 V) with the H<sup>+</sup>/H<sub>2</sub>O/O<sub>2</sub> couple (E&deg; = +1.23 V) at the anode, which species is oxidized preferentially?",choices:["OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) (effectively water), since it has the lower E&deg;, producing O<sub>2</sub>(g)","H<sup>+</sup>(aq), since it has the lower E&deg;","Neither; no oxidation occurs at the anode","Both couples are oxidized in equal amounts"],explain:"The lower E&deg; belongs to the OH<sup>&minus;</sup>/H<sub>2</sub>O/O<sub>2</sub> couple (+0.40 V vs +1.23 V), so OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) (effectively water) is oxidized preferentially, producing O<sub>2</sub>(g) at the anode."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the overall equation for the electrolysis of dilute NaOH(aq), and in what volume ratio do the gaseous products form?",choices:["2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g), in a 2:1 ratio of H<sub>2</sub> to O<sub>2</sub>","2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g), in a 1:2 ratio of H<sub>2</sub> to O<sub>2</sub>","2NaOH(l) &rarr; 2Na(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) + H<sub>2</sub>(g), in a 1:1 ratio","H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g), in a 1:1 ratio"],explain:"Overall, only water is discharged: 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; 2H<sub>2</sub>(g) + O<sub>2</sub>(g), giving a 2:1 volume ratio of H<sub>2</sub> to O<sub>2</sub>, consistent with the stoichiometry of the equation."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why is dilute NaOH(aq) or H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) added to pure water before attempting electrolysis?",choices:["It provides mobile ions to carry the current without itself being consumed, since only water is actually discharged","It is consumed to produce additional H<sub>2</sub>(g) and O<sub>2</sub>(g)","It lowers the boiling point of water so electrolysis can occur at room temperature","It prevents any gas from being produced at either electrode"],explain:"Pure water alone contains too few ions to conduct electricity appreciably; dilute NaOH(aq) or H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) provides mobile ions to carry the current without itself being consumed, since only water is actually discharged at both electrodes."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In the electrolysis of dilute NaCl(aq), which product forms at the anode, and why?",choices:["O<sub>2</sub>(g), since water's E&deg; (+1.23 V) is lower than Cl<sub>2</sub>/Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>'s E&deg; (+1.36 V), so water is oxidized preferentially","Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), since Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> always has the lower E&deg;","H<sub>2</sub>(g), since hydrogen is produced at both electrodes","Na(s), since sodium is discharged preferentially at low concentration"],explain:"In dilute NaCl(aq), water's E&deg; (+1.23 V) is lower than that of the Cl<sub>2</sub>/Cl<sup>&minus;</sup> couple (+1.36 V), so by E&deg; alone, water is oxidized preferentially, giving O<sub>2</sub>(g) at the anode."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why does the anode product change to Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) when concentrated (rather than dilute) NaCl(aq) is electrolyzed?",choices:["The very high concentration of Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) outweighs the small E&deg; disadvantage, so Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized instead of water","Concentrated NaCl(aq) has a different E&deg; value for water than dilute NaCl(aq)","Chlorine gas cannot form in dilute solution under any conditions","The electrode material automatically changes with concentration"],explain:"In concentrated brine, the very high concentration of Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) outweighs the small E&deg; disadvantage relative to water, so Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized instead, giving Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) &ndash; this is the basis of the industrial chlor-alkali process."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What three products are formed simultaneously by the industrial chlor-alkali process (electrolysis of concentrated NaCl(aq))?",choices:["H<sub>2</sub>(g), Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), and NaOH(aq)","Na(s), Cl<sub>2</sub>(g), and H<sub>2</sub>O(l)","H<sub>2</sub>(g), O<sub>2</sub>(g), and NaCl(s)","Na(s), H<sub>2</sub>(g), and O<sub>2</sub>(g)"],explain:"The chlor-alkali process electrolyzes concentrated brine to yield H<sub>2</sub>(g) at the cathode (from water, since Na<sup>+</sup> is not discharged), Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) at the anode (from the high concentration of Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>), and NaOH(aq) accumulating in solution."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In the electrolysis of CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) with inert (graphite) electrodes, what occurs at the cathode, and why?",choices:["Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Cu(s), since Cu<sup>2+</sup> (E&deg; = +0.34 V) is preferred over reducing water (E&deg; = &minus;0.83 V)","2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; H<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2OH<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), since water is always reduced preferentially","SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) is reduced at the cathode","No reaction occurs at the cathode with graphite electrodes"],explain:"With inert electrodes, Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Cu(s) (E&deg; = +0.34 V) is preferred at the cathode over reducing water (E&deg; = &minus;0.83 V), depositing copper metal."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In the electrolysis of CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) with inert (graphite) electrodes, what occurs at the anode, and what is the overall equation?",choices:["Water is oxidized (E&deg; = +1.23 V, preferred over SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>'s much higher E&deg;), giving O<sub>2</sub>(g); overall 2Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; 2Cu(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq)","SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized directly to SO<sub>3</sub>(g)","Cu(s) is oxidized at the anode, exactly as with a copper electrode","No reaction occurs at an inert anode"],explain:"At the inert anode, water (E&deg; = +1.23 V) is preferred for oxidation over SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) (a much higher, unfavourable E&deg;), giving O<sub>2</sub>(g); overall: 2Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; 2Cu(s) + O<sub>2</sub>(g) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In the electrolysis of CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) using copper electrodes (instead of inert graphite), what changes at the anode?",choices:["The copper metal itself is oxidized, Cu(s) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>, instead of water or SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup> being discharged","Water is still oxidized preferentially, exactly as with graphite electrodes","SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized to SO<sub>3</sub>(g) instead of water","The cathode reaction also changes to deposit less copper"],explain:"With copper electrodes, the copper metal itself is oxidized at the anode, Cu(s) &rarr; Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup>, since this is far easier than discharging water or SO<sub>4</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>(aq); the cathode reaction is unchanged."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the net effect of electrolyzing CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) with copper electrodes on the concentration of Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) in solution?",choices:["No change in [Cu<sup>2+</sup>], since copper is simply transferred from anode to cathode","[Cu<sup>2+</sup>] steadily increases over time","[Cu<sup>2+</sup>] steadily decreases over time, reaching zero","[Cu<sup>2+</sup>] oscillates periodically"],explain:"With copper electrodes, the net effect is simply the transfer of copper from anode (dissolving) to cathode (depositing), with no overall change in [Cu<sup>2+</sup>] in solution."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What industrial application relies on the electrolysis of CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) with copper electrodes, where copper is transferred from anode to cathode?",choices:["Electro-refining, used to purify copper industrially","The Hall&ndash;H&eacute;roult process, used to extract aluminium","The chlor-alkali process, used to produce chlorine gas","Anodizing, used to protect aluminium from corrosion"],explain:"This is the basis of electro-refining, used to purify copper industrially: an impure anode dissolves, pure copper deposits on the cathode, while impurities fall away as sludge."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the relationship between charge (Q), current (I), and time (t) used in Faraday's laws of electrolysis?",choices:["Q = It","Q = I/t","Q = I + t","Q = I<sup>2</sup>t"],explain:"The quantity of charge passed is given by Q = It, where Q is in coulombs (C), I is the current in amperes (A), and t is the time in seconds (s)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: What is the correct relationship between moles of electrons transferred, n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>), the charge Q, and the Faraday constant F?",choices:["n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = Q / F","n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = Q &times; F","n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = F / Q","n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = Q + F"],explain:"Since F is the charge on one mole of electrons, the number of moles of electrons transferred is n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = Q / F."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Why must the relevant half-equation always be consulted to convert from moles of electrons to moles of product?",choices:["Because the number of electrons per mole of product depends entirely on the stoichiometry of the half-equation (e.g. 2 e<sup>&minus;</sup> per Cu, but 1 e<sup>&minus;</sup> per Ag, or 4 e<sup>&minus;</sup> per O<sub>2</sub>)","Because Q = It already gives moles of product directly","Because the Faraday constant changes depending on the product formed","Because half-equations are only needed when gases are produced"],explain:"The relationship Q = nF gives the moles of electrons transferred, not directly the moles of product; the conversion depends entirely on the stoichiometry of the relevant half-equation, which varies by substance (2e<sup>&minus;</sup> per Cu, 1e<sup>&minus;</sup> per Ag, 4e<sup>&minus;</sup> per O<sub>2</sub>)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: A current of 2.50 A is passed through CuSO<sub>4</sub>(aq) for exactly 1 hour (3600 s). What quantity of charge, Q, is passed?",choices:["9000 C","2.50 C","3600 C","9.00 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> C"],explain:"Q = It = 2.50 A &times; 3600 s = 9000 C."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Using Q = 9000 C and F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, what amount, in mol, of electrons was transferred?",choices:["0.0933 mol","0.933 mol","9.33 mol","0.00933 mol"],explain:"n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = Q/F = 9000 / 96 485 = 0.0933 mol."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Given n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = 0.0933 mol and the half-equation Cu<sup>2+</sup>(aq) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Cu(s), what amount, in mol, of Cu is deposited at the cathode?",choices:["0.0466 mol","0.0933 mol","0.187 mol","0.0233 mol"],explain:"Since 2 mol of electrons deposit 1 mol of Cu, n(Cu) = n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>)/2 = 0.0933/2 = 0.0466 mol."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Using n(Cu) = 0.0466 mol and M(Cu) = 63.55 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, what mass of copper is deposited at the cathode?",choices:["2.96 g","1.48 g","5.92 g","0.0466 g"],explain:"m(Cu) = n &times; M = 0.0466 mol &times; 63.55 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> = 2.96 g."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Using the same Q = 9000 C (n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = 0.0933 mol) passed through dilute H<sub>2</sub>SO<sub>4</sub>(aq) with inert electrodes, and the anode half-equation 2H<sub>2</sub>O(l) &rarr; O<sub>2</sub>(g) + 4H<sup>+</sup>(aq) + 4e<sup>&minus;</sup>, what amount, in mol, of O<sub>2</sub>(g) is produced?",choices:["0.0233 mol","0.0466 mol","0.0933 mol","0.373 mol"],explain:"Since 4 mol of electrons produce 1 mol of O<sub>2</sub>, n(O<sub>2</sub>) = n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>)/4 = 0.0933/4 = 0.0233 mol."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Using n(O<sub>2</sub>) = 0.0233 mol and molar volume V<sub>m</sub> = 22.7 dm<sup>3</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> (at 273 K, 100 kPa), what volume of O<sub>2</sub>(g) is produced?",choices:["0.529 dm<sup>3</sup> (529 cm<sup>3</sup>)","0.233 dm<sup>3</sup>","1.06 dm<sup>3</sup>","22.7 dm<sup>3</sup>"],explain:"V(O<sub>2</sub>) = n &times; V<sub>m</sub> = 0.0233 mol &times; 22.7 dm<sup>3</sup> mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> = 0.529 dm<sup>3</sup>, equivalent to 529 cm<sup>3</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: A current of 1.50 A is passed through molten AlCl<sub>3</sub> for 2.00 hours (7200 s). What quantity of charge is passed?",choices:["1.08 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> C","3000 C","10 800 C per second","1.50 C"],explain:"Q = It = 1.50 A &times; 7200 s = 10 800 C = 1.08 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> C."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Using Q = 1.08 &times; 10<sup>4</sup> C and F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, how many moles of electrons are transferred, and (given Al<sup>3+</sup> + 3e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Al, M(Al) = 27.0 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) what mass of aluminium is deposited?",choices:["0.1119 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 1.01 g Al","0.1119 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 3.02 g Al","0.336 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 9.07 g Al","1.119 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 30.2 g Al"],explain:"n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = 10 800/96 485 = 0.1119 mol. Since 3 mol of electrons deposit 1 mol of Al, n(Al) = 0.1119/3 = 0.0373 mol, so m(Al) = 0.0373 &times; 27.0 = 1.01 g."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: A current of 5.00 A is passed through molten NaCl for 1930 s (so Q = 9650 C, almost exactly 0.1 F). How many moles of electrons are transferred?",choices:["0.100 mol","1.00 mol","0.0100 mol","10.0 mol"],explain:"n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = Q/F = 9650/96 485 = 0.100 mol."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Using n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = 0.100 mol passed through molten NaCl, and Na<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Na (1 electron per Na), what mass of sodium is deposited (M(Na) = 23.0 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>)?",choices:["2.30 g","1.15 g","4.60 g","0.100 g"],explain:"Since 1 mol of electrons deposits 1 mol of Na, n(Na) = 0.100 mol, so m(Na) = 0.100 &times; 23.0 = 2.30 g."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For the same 0.100 mol of electrons passed through molten NaCl, using 2Cl<sup>&minus;</sup>(l) &rarr; Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> (2 electrons per Cl<sub>2</sub>), what amount of Cl<sub>2</sub>(g) is produced?",choices:["0.0500 mol","0.100 mol","0.200 mol","0.0250 mol"],explain:"Since 2 mol of electrons produce 1 mol of Cl<sub>2</sub>, n(Cl<sub>2</sub>) = 0.100/2 = 0.0500 mol."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: A silver object is electroplated using Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Ag(s) with a current of 0.500 A for 965 s (Q = 482.5 C). How many moles of electrons are transferred, and what mass of Ag (M = 108 g mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>) is deposited?",choices:["0.00500 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 0.540 g Ag","0.0100 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 1.08 g Ag","0.00500 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 0.270 g Ag","0.0500 mol e<sup>&minus;</sup>; 5.40 g Ag"],explain:"n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) = 482.5/96 485 = 0.00500 mol. Since 1 mol of electrons deposits 1 mol of Ag, n(Ag) = 0.00500 mol, so m(Ag) = 0.00500 &times; 108 = 0.540 g."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: In a comparison of copper deposition (Cu<sup>2+</sup> + 2e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Cu) and silver deposition (Ag<sup>+</sup> + e<sup>&minus;</sup> &rarr; Ag) using the same quantity of charge, which metal is deposited in the greater number of moles?",choices:["Silver, since each mole of Ag requires only 1 mole of electrons, compared with 2 moles for Cu","Copper, since copper has a higher molar mass","Both are deposited in exactly equal molar amounts","Neither; charge alone determines mass directly without reference to moles"],explain:"For the same quantity of charge, n(e<sup>&minus;</sup>) is fixed; since Ag requires only 1 electron per ion while Cu requires 2, twice as many moles of Ag are deposited as moles of Cu for the same charge passed."},
{ch:20,q:"In a voltaic cell, is the reaction spontaneous or non-spontaneous, and what is the sign of E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and &Delta;G&deg;?",choices:["Spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &gt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &lt; 0","Non-spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &lt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &gt; 0","Spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &lt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &gt; 0","Non-spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &gt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &lt; 0"],explain:"A voltaic cell runs a spontaneous reaction, with E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &gt; 0 and &Delta;G&deg; &lt; 0, converting chemical energy into electrical energy."},
{ch:20,q:"In an electrolytic cell, is the reaction spontaneous or non-spontaneous, and what is the sign of E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and &Delta;G&deg;?",choices:["Non-spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &lt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &gt; 0, driven by an external supply","Spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &gt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &lt; 0","Non-spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &gt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &lt; 0","Spontaneous; E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &lt; 0, &Delta;G&deg; &gt; 0"],explain:"An electrolytic cell drives a non-spontaneous reaction, with E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> &lt; 0 and &Delta;G&deg; &gt; 0, using an external electrical supply to force the reaction, converting electrical energy into chemical energy."},
{ch:20,q:"What is the polarity of the anode in a voltaic cell, compared with the polarity of the anode in an electrolytic cell?",choices:["Negative in a voltaic cell; positive in an electrolytic cell","Positive in a voltaic cell; negative in an electrolytic cell","Negative in both cell types","Positive in both cell types"],explain:"In a voltaic cell the anode is negative (spontaneously releasing electrons); in an electrolytic cell the anode is made positive deliberately by the external power source, to pull electrons away and force oxidation there."},
{ch:20,q:"Why is the anode made positive deliberately in an electrolytic cell, even though this is the opposite of a voltaic cell's anode polarity?",choices:["To pull electrons away from the anode, forcing oxidation to happen there even though it would not occur on its own","To attract electrons toward the anode so that reduction can occur there instead","Because the external power source has no effect on electrode polarity","Because electrolytic cells never actually involve oxidation at the anode"],explain:"In an electrolytic cell, the anode is made positive deliberately by the external power source, in order to pull electrons away from it and force oxidation to happen there even though it is energetically unfavourable (non-spontaneous) on its own."},
{ch:20,q:"What is true of a cell exactly at equilibrium (a 'dead battery')?",choices:["E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = 0 and &Delta;G&deg; = 0","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> is at its maximum positive value","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> is highly negative","&Delta;G&deg; is highly negative while E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = 0"],explain:"A cell exactly at equilibrium (a 'dead battery') has E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = 0 and &Delta;G&deg; = 0, since there is no longer any net driving force for the reaction in either direction."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For a cell made of Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq)/Ag(s), E&deg; = +0.80 V, and Fe<sup>2+</sup>(aq)/Fe(s), E&deg; = &minus;0.44 V, what is E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and which half-cell is the anode?",choices:["E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +1.24 V; Fe<sup>2+</sup>/Fe is the anode","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;1.24 V; Ag<sup>+</sup>/Ag is the anode","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.36 V; Fe<sup>2+</sup>/Fe is the anode","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +1.24 V; Ag<sup>+</sup>/Ag is the anode"],explain:"Ag<sup>+</sup>/Ag has the higher E&deg;, so it is the cathode; Fe<sup>2+</sup>/Fe (lower E&deg;) is the anode. E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> = (+0.80) &minus; (&minus;0.44) = +1.24 V."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For a cell made of I<sub>2</sub>(s)/I<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), E&deg; = +0.54 V, and Br<sub>2</sub>(l)/Br<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq), E&deg; = +1.07 V, what is E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for the spontaneous reaction, and what is oxidized?",choices:["E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.53 V; I<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized to I<sub>2</sub>(s)","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;0.53 V; Br<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized to Br<sub>2</sub>(l)","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +1.61 V; I<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.53 V; Br<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) is oxidized to Br<sub>2</sub>(l)"],explain:"Br<sub>2</sub>/Br<sup>&minus;</sup> has the higher E&deg;, so it is the cathode (reduced); I<sub>2</sub>/I<sup>&minus;</sup> is the anode, with I<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) oxidized to I<sub>2</sub>(s). E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = (+1.07) &minus; (+0.54) = +0.53 V."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For the reaction Br<sub>2</sub>(l) + 2I<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) &rarr; 2Br<sup>&minus;</sup>(aq) + I<sub>2</sub>(s), with E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.53 V and n = 2 mol, what is &Delta;G&deg; (using F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>)?",choices:["&minus;102 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+102 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;51.1 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;204 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;(2)(96 485)(0.53) = &minus;102 274 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> &asymp; &minus;102 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: A student calculates E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> for a proposed reaction as E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> instead of E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub>. What is the effect of this error?",choices:["It gives a result of the same magnitude but opposite sign to the correct E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, potentially misidentifying a spontaneous reaction as non-spontaneous","It gives exactly the correct answer, since subtraction order does not matter","It only affects the units of E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>, not its magnitude or sign","It has no effect, since E&deg; values are always positive"],explain:"Swapping the order of subtraction negates the result, since E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> = &minus;(E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub>); this is the single highest-risk error in this topic, and would misidentify a genuinely spontaneous reaction (positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>) as non-spontaneous (negative), or vice versa."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For a half-cell with E&deg; = &minus;0.40 V (cathode, since it is the less negative of the pair) and another with E&deg; = &minus;1.66 V (anode), what is the correctly calculated E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>?",choices:["+1.26 V","&minus;1.26 V","+2.06 V","&minus;2.06 V"],explain:"E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = E&deg;<sub>cathode</sub> &minus; E&deg;<sub>anode</sub> = (&minus;0.40) &minus; (&minus;1.66) = &minus;0.40 + 1.66 = +1.26 V, correctly positive since the half-cell with the higher (less negative) E&deg; was identified as the cathode."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Given Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq)/Al(s), E&deg; = &minus;1.66 V, and Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq)/Ag(s), E&deg; = +0.80 V, calculate E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> and identify the spontaneous overall reaction.",choices:["E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +2.46 V; Al(s) + 3Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + 3Ag(s)","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;2.46 V; Al(s) + 3Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + 3Ag(s)","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +2.46 V; Ag(s) + Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) + Al(s)","E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +0.86 V; Al(s) + Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + Ag(s)"],explain:"Ag<sup>+</sup>/Ag has the higher E&deg;, so it is the cathode; Al<sup>3+</sup>/Al (lower E&deg;) is the anode. E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = (+0.80) &minus; (&minus;1.66) = +2.46 V, for the spontaneous reaction Al(s) + 3Ag<sup>+</sup>(aq) &rarr; Al<sup>3+</sup>(aq) + 3Ag(s) (balanced so electrons cancel: 3 &times; Ag half-equation matches 1 &times; Al half-equation)."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: For the Al/Ag cell above, with E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = +2.46 V and n = 3 mol of electrons transferred (from Al &rarr; Al<sup>3+</sup> + 3e<sup>&minus;</sup>), what is &Delta;G&deg; (F = 96 485 C mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>)?",choices:["&minus;712 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","+712 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;237 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>","&minus;1424 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>"],explain:"&Delta;G&deg; = &minus;nFE&deg;<sub>cell</sub> = &minus;(3)(96 485)(2.46) &asymp; &minus;712 000 J mol<sup>&minus;1</sup> = &minus;712 kJ mol<sup>&minus;1</sup>, a very large negative value consistent with the highly favourable, large positive E&deg;<sub>cell</sub>."},
{ch:20,q:"HL: Which statement correctly summarizes the overall HL extension of E&deg; and E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> to systems beyond simple metal displacement, such as MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> and Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>?",choices:["E&deg; data allows the spontaneity of any proposed redox reaction &ndash; including between non-metals or polyatomic ions with no place in a simple metal activity series &ndash; to be predicted quantitatively","E&deg; data can only be applied to reactions involving two different metals","Polyatomic oxidizing agents such as MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> cannot be assigned a standard electrode potential","E&deg; data predicts reaction rate but not reaction direction"],explain:"The electrochemical series and the E&deg;<sub>cell</sub> equation extend to any species that can be oxidized or reduced, including polyatomic ions such as MnO<sub>4</sub><sup>&minus;</sup> and Cr<sub>2</sub>O<sub>7</sub><sup>2&minus;</sup>, allowing the direction and magnitude of electron transfer to be predicted quantitatively for any proposed reaction."}
]
